Step by step
1. Copy code 1 from Markdown into Blender -> Scripting
2. Analyse data given in the csv. file generated from the python console 
3. If missing data, follow your matching path in the BPMN diagram 
4. Our matching patch was to import a generated csv file with indoor conditions to blender. Step 4 is then to copy code 2 from Markdown into Blender -> Scripting. 
It is important that after running code 2, the ifc file in blender is saved with the given information from the imported csv file. If not the next step will not be possible.
5. Now run code 1 again and read the csv file to control that the imported data is now existing in the model. Code 1 is noe modified based on code 2 and the input given for blender. 
Therefor we use code 3 instead, which has the same list of data search in the code, but modified for further search. 
6. Open the new csv file generated with code 3 in excel and control that the imported data is now saved in the model. 
6. Control with Danish Standard if values are within the criteria
7. If not within required values, suggest an improvement or addition for passive or active cooling/heating. 
